# Response formatting

## Scope

- Applies only to the prose of the chat reply to the operator, in every harness (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor).
- Never applies to text passed as a tool argument, written to a file, committed, or posted to an external system (Jira, Slack, GitLab/GitHub). Those follow "Client and analyst bodies" in BASE §1.
- A skill template for a chat reply keeps its sections, fields, and order; its lists, markers, and spacing follow this rule. A skill template for a body that leaves the chat (Jira, Slack, PR, file, commit) stays verbatim.
- A draft of an external body shown for approval is reproduced verbatim in its destination format, inside a quote or code block. Only the text around it follows this layout.
- Subagent reports are exempt.

## Blocks

Fixed order. Include a block only when it has content.

1. Answer: first line, no heading.
2. Blockers
3. Changes / Evidence
4. Findings / Options
5. Not verified
6. Next: always last.

By reply type: simple question uses 1; small task 1, 3, 5, 6; technical decision 1, 4, 6; complex analysis any, with 2 first.
Use `##` headings only when the reply has two or more blocks or runs past about 10 lines.

## Markers

Line prefixes. One meaning each.

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 🚫 | Blocker: stop before proceeding |
| ⚠️ | Risk, not blocking |
| ✅ | Verified or passed, with observed evidence |
| ⛔ | Failed, followed by the deciding error |
| ❓ | Question the operator must answer |
| 👉 | Next action |
| 💡 | Insight that changes a decision. At most one per reply. No unverified numbers |
| 🔹 | New finding, when no marker above fits |
| 🔸 | Still pending or undecided, when no marker above fits |

- Every marker line starts with 4 `&nbsp;`, then the marker, then `&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;` plus one normal space before the text.
- Spacers are the raw `&nbsp;` entity text, because plain spaces collapse. The backticks in this file only name the entity: in a live reply never put a spacer inside backticks, code spans, commands, paths, or code blocks (it renders as visible text, e.g. wrong: `` • `&nbsp;`CLI ``; stored templates excepted, below); backticks are only for real code, paths, commands, and identifiers, and never indent with a literal U+00A0 or other Unicode space (copied, it breaks shells and YAML).
- Correct marker line, typed exactly like this outside any code span:

  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;✅&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tests passed.

- Never start a marker or bullet line with plain spaces: 4 leading spaces render as a code block. A Claude Code Stop hook (`hooks/response-format-check.py`) blocks such a reply.
- Put one spacer paragraph before the 👉 line: a line containing only `&nbsp;`, with a blank line on each side.
- Never use markers in code blocks or tables. Exception: a chat-reply template stored in a skill or KB file may sit in a fenced block with its markers and `&nbsp;`.

## Lists

- No Markdown list syntax (`-`, `*`, `1.`, `1)`): it loses its markers when copied.
- Levels use `•`, `◦`, `▪`, one item per paragraph (a blank line between items).
- A level-N bullet line starts with 6 + 8×(N−1) `&nbsp;` (6, 14, 22), then the bullet, then `&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;` plus one normal space, then the text.
- An emoji list item starts with 4 `&nbsp;`, then the marker, then `&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;` plus one normal space. The narrower bullet gets 2 more before and 1 more after so marker and text line up with emoji items.
- In a single-level list, a marker from the table replaces the `•`. 🔹 and 🔸 apply only when no other marker fits.
- Sequential steps use `Step N:` or A-codes, not numbered lists.

## Option lists

Classify exclusive vs combinable **before** choosing the glyph. The glyph is the exclusivity signal. Every option-list item is an O-code. A-codes are for actions (BASE §5, sequential steps), not items on this list.

- Mutually exclusive (choosing one **forbids** the others): keycaps 1️⃣ to 🔟, in order, as `1️⃣ O1: …`. The recommended option comes first and says "Recommended". 👉 reply format: `"1"` or `"O2"`.
- Combinable (the operator may take more than one): circled letters Ⓐ to Ⓩ in text form, as `Ⓐ O1: …`. Default to this when both readings are possible. 👉 reply format: `"A C"` or `"ALL"`; offering either forbids keycaps, and `"1"` never sits beside `"ALL"`.
- Neither "Recommended" nor the numeral inside `O1` makes a list exclusive. Never pair a glyph with an A-code (`1️⃣ A1`, `Ⓐ A1`).
- Both use the emoji-marker spacing. A ❓ line states the question before the list.
- More than 10 exclusive options: narrow or split the question instead of extending the list.

## Emphasis

- Bold at most one or two items per block.
- Reply keywords the operator can send are uppercase and wrapped in double quotes: "GO AHEAD", "KEEP", "REVERT", option codes like "O1". They sit mostly on 👉 and ❓ lines. Other imperative verbs keep normal case and take no quotes.
- Never uppercase commands, aliases, paths, or code; they stay in backticks with their real case.
